---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Verify a Wakir Labs commit";
const description =
  "How a third party verifies any commit in the Wakir Labs history with OpenTimestamps and Bitcoin.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="essay">
  <header class="essay__header">
    <h1 class="essay__title">Verify a Wakir Labs commit</h1>
    <p class="essay__lead">
    Commits in <em>this</em> repository — and in our internal governance
    history — are timestamped against the Bitcoin blockchain via
    <a href="https://opentimestamps.org">OpenTimestamps</a>. The proof is a
    small <code>.ots</code> file, checked in next to the commit hash. Those
    two things are all you need: with <em>a commit hash</em> and <em>its
    matching <code>.ots</code> file</em>, anyone can establish — without
    trusting us — that the commit existed no later than a specific Bitcoin
    block.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="essay__body">

  <p>
    This is the public side of <em>Proof-of-History in practice</em>. It
    is what we mean when we say the documentation is anchored.
  </p>

  <h2>What this covers</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>In this repository, all 60 proof files carry a Bitcoin
    attestation</strong> — a block, not a calendar promise. 46 of them only
    reached that state on 2026-09-15; they had been sitting unconfirmed in
    the calendar servers since May. Re-checked 2026-09-20.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The three code repositories carry none.</strong>
    <code>wakir-runtime</code>, <code>wakir-protocol</code> and
    <code>wakir-verify</code> have no <code>meta/timestamps/</code>
    directory at all (measured 2026-09-15). An earlier version of this page
    claimed every commit in every Wakir Labs repository was timestamped. It
    was not. Whether to extend commit stamping to those repositories is an
    open decision; until it is made, this page describes what exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Three steps</h2>

  <h3>1. Install the OpenTimestamps client</h3>

  <p>
    The reference client is <code>ots</code>, distributed as a Python
    package. On any platform with Python 3.8+:
  </p>

  <pre><code>pip install opentimestamps-client</code></pre>

  <p>
    Verify the install:
  </p>

  <pre><code>ots --version</code></pre>

  <h3>2. Get the commit and its <code>.ots</code> proof</h3>

  <p>
    Clone the repository you want to verify. The proof files live under
    <code>meta/timestamps/&lt;short-hash&gt;.ots</code>:
  </p>

  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/wakir-labs/site.git
cd site
ls meta/timestamps/</code></pre>

  <p>
    Each file is the <code>.ots</code> attestation for one commit,
    indexed by the commit's short hash.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Run <code>ots verify</code></h3>

  <p>
    For a worked example, take commit <code>82d40f7</code> from this
    site's history (<em>"feat(about): add EN+DE about pages"</em>):
  </p>

  <pre><code># From inside the cloned repo:
printf "%s\n" "$(git rev-parse 82d40f7)" &gt; /tmp/commit.txt
ots verify -f /tmp/commit.txt meta/timestamps/82d40f7.ots</code></pre>

  <p>
    The hook stamps the 40-character commit hash <em>as ASCII text, with a
    trailing newline</em> — which is exactly what <code>printf "%s\n"</code>
    writes. Two earlier versions of this page told you to feed the raw hash
    bytes (<code>xxd -r -p</code>) instead. That is not what was stamped, so
    <code>ots</code> rejects it with <code>File does not match
    original!</code>. We found this on 2026-09-15 by running
    this page's own worked example end to end. Both published forms are
    corrected.
  </p>

  <p>
    A successful verification prints something like:
  </p>

  <pre><code>Success! Bitcoin block N attests existence as of YYYY-MM-DD</code></pre>

  <p>
    That is the receipt. The commit existed at or before that Bitcoin
    block. No trust in us required — only in Bitcoin and in the
    OpenTimestamps calendar servers, which only sign; they do not
    back-date.
  </p>

  <h2>The corporate repository</h2>

  <p>
    The full corporate repository — ADRs, activity log, governance — is
    currently <strong>local to the supervisory board</strong>, not yet
    public. Public verification today therefore covers the
    <code>wakir-labs/*</code> repositories on GitHub
    (<a href="https://github.com/wakir-labs">github.com/wakir-labs</a>).
    When the corporate repository becomes public, the same recipe
    applies: clone, point <code>ots verify</code> at the
    <code>.ots</code> file and the commit hash, read the receipt.
  </p>

  <p>
    Members of the supervisory board verify against their local clone of
    the corporate repository the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Advanced: verify against your own Bitcoin node</h2>

  <p>
    By default the OpenTimestamps client reaches out to public calendar
    servers and, for the Bitcoin attestation, to a remote block explorer.
    If you run your own Bitcoin node, you can verify the proof end-to-end
    against your own copy of the chain:
  </p>

  <pre><code>ots --bitcoin-node http://user:pass@127.0.0.1:8332 verify -f /tmp/commit.txt meta/timestamps/82d40f7.ots</code></pre>

  <p>
    This removes the last residual trust assumption: you are now reading
    the attestation directly from a chain you validate yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>If verification fails</h2>

  <p>
    Two normal reasons:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The proof is "pending".</strong> Fresh commits live in the
      calendar servers for a few hours before being aggregated into a
      Bitcoin block. Run <code>ots upgrade meta/timestamps/&lt;hash&gt;.ots</code>
      after a few hours, then re-verify.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You hashed the wrong thing.</strong> The hook stamps the
      40-character commit hash as ASCII text with a trailing newline.
      Reproduce it with
      <code>printf "%s\n" "$(git rev-parse &lt;commit&gt;)"</code> —
      not with <code>xxd -r -p</code>. Earlier versions of this page
      recommended the latter; they were wrong.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If neither applies, <a href="https://github.com/wakir-labs/site/issues">open an issue</a>.
    A failed verification on a real commit is a finding we want to know
    about.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/de/verify/">Auf Deutsch lesen</a>.
  </p>
  </div>
  </article>
</Base>
